<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southern Cameroons</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1922–1961</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroons</a> was the southern part of the British Cameroons, a mandated and later trust territory administered with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buea">Buea</a> as its centre. It gained its own government in the 1950s. In the plebiscite of February 1961 it voted to join the former French Cameroun, forming the Federal Republic of Cameroon, the origin of today's English-speaking regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons.jpg" alt="Southern Cameroons" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hogweard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_British_Cameroon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
